tion.\n   - Consider the scenario where George likes music (\\( L \\)) and has access to a program (\\( P \\)):\n     - \\( L \\rightarrow W \\) implies \\( W \\) (George wants to compose).\n     - \\( P \\rightarrow C \\) implies \\( C \\) (George can compose).\n     - \\( (C \\land W) \\rightarrow F \\) implies \\( F \\) (George will compose).\n\n   - Now, let\'s consider the scenario where George will not compose (\\( \\neg F \\)):\n     - If George will not compose, then \\( \\neg F \\) is true.\n     - However, from the given information, if George can compose (\\( C \\)) and wants to compose (\\( W \\)), he will compose (\\( F \\)).\n     - Therefore, if George will not compose (\\( \\neg F \\)), it must be because he cannot compose (\\( \\neg C \\)).\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - The statement "If George will not compose, George cannot compose" is consistent with the given information.\n   - Therefore, the statement is true.\n\n<｜Assistant｜>\nBased on the given information, the statement "If George will not compose, George cannot compose" is true. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\\boxed{A}\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
